---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { newQuestions, screenedPaperCount } from '../lib/newQuestions';
const reportCount=1+newQuestions.filter(q=>q.report_run_id).length;
---
<Base title="About the pilot"><article class="article"><p class="eyebrow">ABOUT / SCOPE</p><h1>Small investigations you can inspect.</h1><p class="lede">The Open Questions Observatory links research questions to their sources, practical blockers and bounded investigations. Each result retains the plan and evidence needed to assess its scope.</p>
<h2>What was done</h2><p>The current catalog contains {1+newQuestions.length} questions and {reportCount} research {reportCount===1?'note':'notes'}. The first investigation compared seasonal and ordinary El Niño forecasts with a persistence baseline.</p>
{newQuestions.length>0 && <p>The next cohort screened {screenedPaperCount} papers across five disciplines and produced ten source-linked question cards. Two further attempts investigated finite prefix-reversal cases and river-flow uncertainty calibration. Their outcomes and limitations are recorded separately.</p>}
<h2>What remains uncertain</h2><p>The English-language source sample and targeted literature searches do not certify novelty or establish that a question is universally unanswered. A finite mathematical result has a different evidentiary scope from an empirical comparison. A reproduced inconclusive result remains inconclusive. Each report explains its own data, assumptions and unresolved issues.</p>
<h2>Accountability and review</h2><p>Codex performed the source assessment, implementation, analysis and automated verification. Operator approvals and interpretation reviews are dated in the publication records. Independent scientific novelty and external expert review have not been established.</p>
<h2>Corrections</h2><p>Any later substantive correction should preserve the old result and append a dated notice. A failed reproduction, overlooked prior answer or data problem should change the visible assessment and remove unsupported claims.</p>
<h2>Data credit</h2><p>NOAA Climate Prediction Center; data provided by NOAA Physical Sciences Laboratory, Boulder, Colorado, USA. The first source paper is Gan et al. (2026), arXiv:2601.02050v1, CC BY 4.0.</p>
{newQuestions.length>0 && <p>The streamflow data are derived from Gallant and Wilson's <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/23142989">Zenodo record 23142989</a>, version 1.1.0, CC BY 4.0. Paper citations, brief source quotations and artifact-specific rights are provided in the individual records. Original source full texts are excluded from this site. No author or data-provider endorsement is implied.</p>}
</article></Base>
